Reused evidence
- `E0001` covers `4B.1`, `4B.8`, `4B.10`.
REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0001"],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.8","4B.10"]}
- `E0003` covers `4B.8`.
REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0003"],"lenses":["4B.8"]}

New evidence
- [Congress proclaims BBM-Sara as duly elected president, VP](https://www.congress.gov.ph/index.php/media/photojournal/3001) is the formal transfer-point after the May 9 election.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Congress proclaims BBM-Sara as duly elected president, VP","publisher":"House of Representatives Philippines","publication_date":"2022-05-25","url":"https://www.congress.gov.ph/index.php/media/photojournal/3001","claim":"Congress sitting in joint session proclaimed Ferdinand Marcos Jr. and Sara Duterte as the duly elected president and vice president after the May 9, 2022 elections.","locator":"photo journal summary text","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary legislative record of the formal proclamation after canvass.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 election-transfer evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Directly relevant to Marcos because it records the official constitutional transfer into office.","contrary_evidence":["This establishes formal transfer, not the fairness of all campaign conditions.","It does not by itself show Marcos personally strengthened institutions."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.8","4B.10"]}

- [No reports of 'failure of elections': Comelec](https://www.pna.gov.ph/articles/1174116) supplies the official, favorable counterweight that the polls were generally peaceful and no area had a failure-of-elections classification.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"No reports of 'failure of elections': Comelec","publisher":"Philippine News Agency","publication_date":"2022-05-10","url":"https://www.pna.gov.ph/articles/1174116","claim":"Comelec said no area in the country was classified as a failure of elections and described the May 9 polls as the most peaceful in recent years.","locator":"opening paragraph and Comelec quotation","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"State newswire reporting Comelec's contemporaneous assessment; useful but still a mediated official summary.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 election-period evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Contextual rather than Marcos-specific conduct; it bears on the election environment that produced his victory.","contrary_evidence":["The same election cycle also had reports of vote-buying, disinformation, and harassment.","Official election calm does not eliminate campaign-period manipulation concerns."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.10"]}

- [Final Report of the Philippine Election 2022 International Observers Mission](https://ichrp.net/final-report-of-the-philippine-election-2022-international-observers-mission/) is the strongest adverse election-process source in the packet.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Final Report of the Philippine Election 2022 International Observers Mission","publisher":"International Coalition for Human Rights in the Philippines","publication_date":"2022-06-28","url":"https://ichrp.net/final-report-of-the-philippine-election-2022-international-observers-mission/","claim":"The observer mission said the 2022 elections featured vote buying, electronic voting-counting failures, misinformation, red-tagging, threats, and killings, and it said its observers were themselves harassed and red-tagged.","locator":"mission summary text at lines 63-65","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary mission report from a large independent observer effort with specific documented findings.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 election-period evidence, including the immediate aftermath.","ruler_attribution":"Partly direct and partly contextual; the campaign atmosphere and outcomes shaped Marcos's rise, but not all abuses were personally ordered by him.","contrary_evidence":["The mission is strongly critical and advocacy-aligned.","It reports an environment, not a single direct Marcos instruction for every cited abuse."],"lenses":["4B.2","4B.3","4B.9","4B.10"]}

- [Philippines | Reuters Institute for the Study of Journalism](https://reutersinstitute.politics.ox.ac.uk/digital-news-report/2022/philippines) documents campaign-era hostility to journalists and selective media access.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Philippines | Reuters Institute for the Study of Journalism","publisher":"Reuters Institute for the Study of Journalism","publication_date":"2022-06-15","url":"https://reutersinstitute.politics.ox.ac.uk/digital-news-report/2022/philippines","claim":"The report says concerns over press freedom grew because Marcos held journalists in low regard during the campaign, barred professional journalists from some campaign events, snubbed media-hosted debates, and favored friendly influencers and a partisan TV station.","locator":"lines 32-37","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Research-based journalism study with concrete contemporaneous observations and attribution to Marcos's campaign behavior.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 campaign-period evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Marcos's campaign conduct and media strategy.","contrary_evidence":["The campaign was not a full media blackout; Marcos did still grant some interviews.","This is about campaign behavior more than post-inauguration state policy."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.9"]}

- The same Reuters Institute report also notes coordinated DDoS attacks on major Philippine news sites.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Philippines | Reuters Institute for the Study of Journalism","publisher":"Reuters Institute for the Study of Journalism","publication_date":"2022-06-15","url":"https://reutersinstitute.politics.ox.ac.uk/digital-news-report/2022/philippines","claim":"The report says coordinated DDoS attacks crippled major Philippine news organizations, including Rappler, Vera Files, ABS-CBN, GMA News, CNN Philippines, and others.","locator":"lines 39-40","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Same study, different materially distinct claim with specific list of affected outlets.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 campaign-period evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Contextual rather than directly ordered by Marcos, but it reflects the hostile information environment around his rise.","contrary_evidence":["The report does not attribute every attack to Marcos personally.","It describes environment and incidents, not a completed policy of state censorship."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.9"]}

- [‘A disservice to voters’: Marcos snubs KBP, other presidential interviews to control narrative](https://pcij.org/2022/02/04/marcos-snubs-kbp-other-presidential-interviews-to-control-narrative/) is direct evidence of selective media engagement.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"‘A disservice to voters’: Marcos snubs KBP, other presidential interviews to control narrative","publisher":"Philippine Center for Investigative Journalism","publication_date":"2022-02-04","url":"https://pcij.org/2022/02/04/marcos-snubs-kbp-other-presidential-interviews-to-control-narrative/","claim":"Marcos skipped multiple journalist-led interviews and forums, and observers said he was selecting friendly outlets and trying to control the terms of engagement with the press.","locator":"opening paragraphs and quoted analyst commentary","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Investigative local reporting with direct commentary from media and governance observers.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 campaign-period evidence before the election.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Marcos's campaign choices.","contrary_evidence":["He did not avoid all media interaction outright.","The article includes critical interpretation from commentators, so the inference should stay limited to selective access and narrative control."],"lenses":["4B.2","4B.3","4B.6"]}

- [Inauguration 2022: Marcos silent on attacks vs press freedom](https://pcij.org/2022/07/01/inauguration-2022-marcos-silent-on-attacks-vs-press-freedom/) shows the first inauguration omitted a rights/press-freedom break with the past.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Inauguration 2022: Marcos silent on attacks vs press freedom","publisher":"Philippine Center for Investigative Journalism","publication_date":"2022-07-01","url":"https://pcij.org/2022/07/01/inauguration-2022-marcos-silent-on-attacks-vs-press-freedom/","claim":"PCIJ reported that Marcos's inaugural address was silent on attacks on press freedom, even as his press secretary promised a good relationship with mainstream media.","locator":"lines 89-95","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous local investigative reporting with a clear read of the inaugural speech and immediate media posture.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 inauguration evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Marcos's inaugural messaging and early administration tone.","contrary_evidence":["Silence is not the same as explicit repression.","The administration did voice general friendliness toward mainstream media."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.10"]}

- [Rappler journalist shoved during Uniteam rally](https://cmfr-phil.org/press-freedom-protection/attacks-and-threats-against-the-media/alerts/rappler-journalist-shoved-during-uniteam-rally/) captures campaign staff physically blocking a reporter.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Rappler journalist shoved during Uniteam rally","publisher":"Center for Media Freedom and Responsibility","publication_date":"2022-04-22","url":"https://cmfr-phil.org/press-freedom-protection/attacks-and-threats-against-the-media/alerts/rappler-journalist-shoved-during-uniteam-rally/","claim":"Two Marcos campaign staffers prevented Rappler reporter Lian Buan from interviewing Marcos Jr., and a security officer shoved the reporter while a media officer pushed aside her camera.","locator":"lines 33-36","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent press-freedom monitor with a concrete incident and contemporaneous documentation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 campaign-period evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Marcos's campaign environment and staff conduct.","contrary_evidence":["This is a campaign incident, not a formal state policy.","It does not prove Marcos personally ordered the shove, only that his staff created the access barrier."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.9"]}

- [FOCAP concerned over difficulty accessing Bongbong Marcos, his supporters’ attack on journos](https://www.gmanetwork.com/news/topstories/nation/830504/focap-concerned-over-difficulty-accessing-bongbong-marcos-his-supporters-attack-on-journos/story/) shows broader campaign hostility to the press.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"FOCAP concerned over difficulty accessing Bongbong Marcos, his supporters’ attack on journos","publisher":"GMA News Online","publication_date":"2022-05-03","url":"https://www.gmanetwork.com/news/topstories/nation/830504/focap-concerned-over-difficulty-accessing-bongbong-marcos-his-supporters-attack-on-journos/story/","claim":"FOCAP said Marcos supporters attacked journalists online and that reporters had difficulty getting clear and substantial answers during the campaign, with pro-Marcos content endangering and smearing independent journalists.","locator":"lines 115-120","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Local mainstream reporting quoting an organized press body and specific incidents.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 campaign-period evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly direct to Marcos's campaign milieu; some abuse came from supporters rather than from Marcos personally.","contrary_evidence":["The report focuses on supporters and access issues, not a direct written order from Marcos.","It is evidence of the climate around the campaign, not a formal censorship mechanism."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.9"]}

- [CHR, NUJP urge gov't to address attacks vs. journalists](https://www.gmanetwork.com/news/topstories/nation/847830/nujp-urge-gov-t-to-address-attacks-vs-journalists/story/) shows the threat environment persisted into Marcos's first months in office.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"CHR, NUJP urge gov't to address attacks vs. journalists","publisher":"GMA News Online","publication_date":"2022-10-12","url":"https://www.gmanetwork.com/news/topstories/nation/847830/nujp-urge-gov-t-to-address-attacks-vs-journalists/story/","claim":"The CHR called on the Marcos administration to address attacks on journalists after death threats against TV5 reporters and after the killing of Percy Lapid, and the NUJP challenged the administration to translate its promise to protect journalists into action.","locator":"lines 104-123","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous reporting on CHR and NUJP reactions to concrete threats and a killing during Marcos's first months.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 ruler-period evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Partly direct and partly inherited: it documents what the Marcos administration faced and what it had not yet demonstrably fixed.","contrary_evidence":["The article shows pressure on the administration to respond, not a proven policy of repression by Marcos himself.","It is about threats and accountability rather than a formal censorship law."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.9","4B.10"]}

- [Marcos leads presidential race amid massive disinformation](https://pcij.org/2022/05/08/marcos-presidential-elections-massive-disinformation/) is the clearest local summary of the disinformation advantage around his campaign.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Marcos leads presidential race amid massive disinformation","publisher":"Philippine Center for Investigative Journalism","publication_date":"2022-05-08","url":"https://pcij.org/2022/05/08/marcos-presidential-elections-massive-disinformation/","claim":"PCIJ reported that studies found Marcos was the main beneficiary of false or misleading content on social media and that his campaign relied on historical revisionism and narrative control.","locator":"lines 110-111 and 155-156","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Investigative local reporting synthesizing fact-checking and academic work on the campaign information environment.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 election-period evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Directly tied to Marcos's campaign advantage and media strategy.","contrary_evidence":["The article also describes a preexisting, broader disinformation ecosystem, so not all of it is uniquely attributable to Marcos.","It is still a campaign-environment claim, not a formal state censorship claim."],"lenses":["4B.2","4B.6","4B.10"]}

Search and rejection summary
I screened roughly 30 documents across official Philippine election bodies, congressional records, local investigative reporting, Reuters Institute research, press-freedom monitors, and human-rights groups. I kept the items that materially affected a 4B judgment: formal transfer, election-process legitimacy, campaign narrative control, press access, harassment, and early administration posture.

Removed or deprioritized items fell into these buckets:
- Duplicates: several articles repeated the same red-tagging, journalist access, or disinformation claims.
- Out of period: 2023 and later press-freedom or disinformation articles were informative but not 2022 target-period evidence.
- Irrelevant to 4B: foreign policy, nuclear-energy, and domestic-safety sources that do not bear on political freedom were excluded here.
- Blocked or partial access: some official pages and social posts were not cleanly fetchable; where the direct page failed, I used an accessible mirrored or reported copy instead.
- Overly generalized democracy indices: I did not re-fetch Freedom House, V-Dem, RSF, BTI, or Polity because the local prior already supplies those structured facts and the task asked not to re-fetch structured datasets.

Remaining gaps
- I did not find strong 2022 evidence that Marcos independently strengthened courts, legislatures, audit bodies, or local oversight institutions. `4B.4` remains the thinnest lens.
- I did not find direct 2022 evidence of a major institutional reform that improved political equality for excluded groups or opposition regions under Marcos personally. `4B.7` is still weak.
- The record on surveillance and digital controls is mostly indirect: campaign hostility, disinformation, and platform pressure are visible, but I did not find a Marcos-ordered surveillance or shutdown policy in 2022.
- More search would help mainly on appointment choices, formal media-policy decisions, and any concrete changes to institutional independence after the inauguration.
